# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMC_ADO16
# BMC 1100/1300 (ADO16): the Austin and Morris 1100/1300 volume cars, the
# best-selling car in Britain 1963-1966 and 1968-1971, with 14.3% of the UK
# market at its 1965 peak. Launched as the Morris 1100 on 15 August 1962 (the
# Austin 1100 followed in September 1963); production ended in June 1974.
# 2.3 million were built, over half sold at home. One model with four colours
# covers the whole family: the 1100 (to 1967), the 1300 (from October 1967) and
# the 1971 Mk III were the same body shell and differ only in grille, wheel and
# trim detail, which is below the resolution of a pak-scale private car. The
# front end modelled is the Mk I form (round headlamps, narrow central grille,
# chrome bumpers) that covered most of the 1963-1971 anchor period.
#
# Colours: Trafalgar Blue, Tartan Red, Snowberry White, Connaught Green, El Paso
# Beige. These are real BMC paint names with their BMC/BL codes, taken from
# period Austin-Morris colour charts: the Austin 1100 saloon and Countryman
# "colours and trim" folder, publication 2189F (1966), and the British Leyland
# Austin-Morris/MG/Wolseley/Princess "paint and trim colours" chart, publication
# 2760 (1970). Charts: archived scans at ado16.info. Codes: BU.37, RD.9, WT.4,
# GN.18, BG.17. The 1962-67 BMC off-white was Old English White (WT.3);
# Snowberry White (WT.4) is the name of the later 1960s/1970 white and is used
# here for the cream colourway across the model's life.
#
# Colour shares: the DVSA first-registration table starts with 1994
# registrations, so its measured base for cars first registered in 1994-96
# (blue 23-25%, red 19-23%, white 7-10%, black 8-10%, grey 4-5%, silver 9-11%,
# other 20-25%) is the starting point, adjusted for 1960s registrations where
# the palette was wider: blue 28% (leading colour; the period charts offered
# the widest choice of blues), red 24%, white 16%, green 16% and beige/brown
# 16% (green and beige/brown sit inside the measured "other" group, which was
# far larger before the 1970s; both charts list them). Black (8-10% measured)
# is the next colour and is omitted.
#
# DistributionWeight totals 500 across the five colourways, the 2026-11-15
# anchor convention for a model that was the most popular car on the road at
# its time (provisional; a central rebase follows).
#
# Dates describe when each colour would be seen regularly on UK roads, not
# production dates. Retire dates are estimates from the mid-1980s rust-out of a
# 1960s car with a very large parc; they are not measured.

